<% content_for :title, "Mixing Traditions | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="spirituality-mixing-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="spirituality-mixing-hero">

    <div class="spirituality-mixing-hero-content">

      <p class="spirituality-mixing-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / SPIRITUALITY / MIXING TRADITIONS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>MORE THAN</span>
        <span>ONE FUCKING</span>
        <span>PATH.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="spirituality-mixing-intro">
        BUDDHISM. HINDUISM. CHRISTIANITY.<br>
        DIFFERENT TRADITIONS. DIFFERENT ANSWERS.<br>
        PLENTY TO LEARN.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- CONTENT -->
  <section class="spirituality-mixing-content">


    <!-- 01 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-black">

      <p class="smt-number">01 / MIXING TRADITIONS</p>

      <h2>
        YOU DON'T<br>
        HAVE TO FIT<br>
        IN ONE BOX.
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          Spirituality doesn't have to begin with choosing a team.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can encounter Buddhism and find something powerful
          in mindfulness and non-attachment.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can read the <em>Bhagavad Gita</em> and connect with
          its ideas about action, duty, devotion, and letting go
          of the outcome.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can encounter Christianity and be moved by teachings
          about love, forgiveness, grace, service, and hope.
        </p>

        <p>
          Learning from one tradition doesn't automatically require
          pretending every other tradition has nothing to offer.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 02 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-pink">

      <p class="smt-number">02 / BUT THEY'RE NOT THE SAME</p>

      <h2>
        DON'T BLEND<br>
        EVERYTHING<br>
        INTO SOUP.
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          Finding connections between religions doesn't mean
          erasing their differences.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buddhism, Hinduism, and Christianity developed in different
          places, from different histories, and contain very different
          teachings about the self, God, suffering, salvation,
          liberation, death, and ultimate reality.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-callout">
          RESPECTING DIFFERENCES<br>
          IS PART OF RESPECTING<br>
          THE FUCKING TRADITIONS.
        </div>

        <p>
          You don't need to force every teaching to agree.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes two traditions are asking similar questions.
          Sometimes their answers overlap.
          Sometimes their answers are fundamentally different.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's okay.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 03 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-yellow">

      <p class="smt-number">03 / BUDDHISM</p>

      <h2>
        PAY<br>
        ATTENTION.
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          Buddhism gives me a language for looking directly
          at suffering instead of immediately running from it.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-stack">
          <span>MINDFULNESS</span>
          <span>IMPERMANENCE</span>
          <span>CRAVING</span>
          <span>NON-ATTACHMENT</span>
          <span>COMPASSION</span>
          <span>THE MIDDLE WAY</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          The question becomes:
        </p>

        <div class="smt-thought">
          CAN I BE PRESENT WITH WHAT'S HERE WITHOUT IMMEDIATELY
          TRYING TO ESCAPE IT?
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 04 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-purple">

      <p class="smt-number">04 / HINDUISM</p>

      <h2>
        DO WHAT'S<br>
        YOURS<br>
        TO DO.
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          Hindu traditions give me another set of questions.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-stack">
          <span>DHARMA</span>
          <span>KARMA</span>
          <span>THE SELF</span>
          <span>DEVOTION</span>
          <span>DISCIPLINE</span>
          <span>ACTION WITHOUT ATTACHMENT TO RESULTS</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          The question becomes:
        </p>

        <div class="smt-thought">
          WHAT IS MINE TO DO — AND WHAT OUTCOME AM I TRYING
          TO FUCKING CONTROL?
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 05 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-black">

      <p class="smt-number">05 / CHRISTIANITY</p>

      <h2>
        LOVE.<br>
        GRACE.<br>
        BEGIN AGAIN.
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          Christianity brings its own enormous and diverse tradition,
          centered on the life and teachings of Jesus and relationship
          with God.
        </p>

        <p>
          Even for someone who doesn't interpret every Christian teaching
          in the same way, there are ideas worth wrestling with.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-stack">
          <span>LOVE YOUR NEIGHBOR.</span>
          <span>FORGIVENESS.</span>
          <span>GRACE.</span>
          <span>SERVICE.</span>
          <span>HUMILITY.</span>
          <span>HOPE.</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          The question becomes:
        </p>

        <div class="smt-thought">
          HOW DO I LOVE PEOPLE — INCLUDING MYSELF —
          WHEN PEOPLE ARE FUCKING COMPLICATED?
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 06 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-pink">

      <p class="smt-number">06 / WHERE THEY MEET</p>

      <h2>
        DIFFERENT<br>
        ROADS.<br>
        FAMILIAR QUESTIONS.
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          These traditions don't teach identical things.
        </p>

        <p>
          But they often make us wrestle with some recognizable
          human problems.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-grid">

          <div class="smt-mini-card">
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>SUFFERING</h3>
            <p>
              What do we do when being alive fucking hurts?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="smt-mini-card">
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>DESIRE</h3>
            <p>
              What happens when wanting starts running our lives?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="smt-mini-card">
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>COMPASSION</h3>
            <p>
              How should we respond to suffering in ourselves
              and other people?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="smt-mini-card">
            <span>04</span>
            <h3>ACTION</h3>
            <p>
              What does it mean to live according to something
              bigger than impulse?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="smt-mini-card">
            <span>05</span>
            <h3>IDENTITY</h3>
            <p>
              Who are we beneath everything that changes?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="smt-mini-card">
            <span>06</span>
            <h3>CONNECTION</h3>
            <p>
              What connects us to other people, the world,
              or something beyond ourselves?
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 07 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-yellow">

      <p class="smt-number">07 / WHERE THEY DON'T</p>

      <h2>
        LET THEM<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        DISAGREE.
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          This matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buddhism's teachings about non-self should not simply be
          rewritten as the Hindu concept of Atman.
        </p>

        <p>
          Hindu understandings of ultimate reality shouldn't simply
          be renamed the Christian God.
        </p>

        <p>
          Christian salvation shouldn't automatically be treated
          as another word for Buddhist nirvana or Hindu moksha.
        </p>

        <p>
          Similarities can help us think.
          Differences can help us think too.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-callout">
          YOU DON'T HAVE TO MAKE<br>
          EVERYBODY AGREE<br>
          TO LEARN FROM EVERYBODY.
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 08 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-purple">

      <p class="smt-number">08 / TAKE WHAT SPEAKS TO YOU</p>

      <h2>
        CURIOUS<br>
        DOESN'T MEAN<br>
        CARELESS.
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          There's a difference between respectfully learning from
          another tradition and treating sacred traditions like
          decorations in a spiritual gift shop.
        </p>

        <p>
          If something speaks to you, learn where it came from.
        </p>

        <p>
          Read the actual teachings.
          Learn how practitioners understand them.
          Notice when an idea has been stripped of its original context.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-stack">
          <span>BE CURIOUS.</span>
          <span>ASK QUESTIONS.</span>
          <span>LEARN THE CONTEXT.</span>
          <span>RESPECT THE PEOPLE.</span>
          <span>DON'T PRETEND YOU KNOW WHAT YOU DON'T.</span>
        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 09 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-black">

      <p class="smt-number">09 / BUILDING YOUR PRACTICE</p>

      <h2>
        SO WHAT<br>
        ACTUALLY<br>
        HELPS?
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          Your spiritual practice doesn't have to pass somebody else's
          purity test to be meaningful to you.
        </p>

        <p>
          But it should probably do more than look cool on a shelf.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-practice-grid">

          <div>
            <span>01</span>
            <p>
              Read from traditions you're genuinely interested in.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>02</span>
            <p>
              Keep the original context instead of flattening everything together.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>03</span>
            <p>
              Notice which teachings actually change how you behave.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>04</span>
            <p>
              Question ideas instead of believing them because they sound spiritual.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>05</span>
            <p>
              Allow contradictions to exist without immediately solving them.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>06</span>
            <p>
              Build rituals and practices that remind you how you want to live.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- 10 -->
    <article class="smt-section smt-section-pink">

      <p class="smt-number">10 / YOUR OWN QUESTIONS</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DO<br>
        YOU ACTUALLY<br>
        BELIEVE?
      </h2>

      <div class="smt-copy">

        <p>
          Mixing traditions gets more interesting when it stops being
          a collection of cool ideas and starts making you answer
          your own questions.
        </p>

        <div class="smt-question-list">

          <p>WHAT DO I BELIEVE ABOUT SUFFERING?</p>
          <p>WHAT DO I BELIEVE ABOUT GOD OR THE DIVINE?</p>
          <p>WHAT DO I THINK A PERSON ACTUALLY IS?</p>
          <p>WHAT DO I BELIEVE HAPPENS WHEN WE DIE?</p>
          <p>WHAT DOES FORGIVENESS MEAN TO ME?</p>
          <p>WHAT DOES A GOOD LIFE LOOK LIKE?</p>
          <p>WHAT PRACTICES ACTUALLY MAKE ME LIVE DIFFERENTLY?</p>
          <p>WHAT DON'T I FUCKING KNOW?</p>

        </div>

        <p>
          "I don't know" is allowed to be an answer.
        </p>

      </div>

    </article>


    <!-- ENDING -->
    <article class="smt-ending">

      <p>
        YOU DON'T NEED TO FORCE YOUR SPIRITUALITY<br>
        INTO SOMEBODY ELSE'S FUCKING BOX.
      </p>

      <h2>
        LEARN.<br>
        QUESTION.<br>
        BUILD.
      </h2>

      <p class="smt-ending-copy">
        Take what changes you. Respect where it came from.
        Leave room for mystery.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO SPIRITUALITY",
          spirituality_path,
          class: "smt-back-link" %>

    </article>

  </section>

</main>